     EXPLANATION OF OPTION
 
     Use the "Delete" logical unit option to delete a logical unit to 
     complete one of the following tasks:
 
          - Changing the RAID level of a logical unit
          - Changing the drives in a logical unit
          - Deleting a drive from the array.
 
     If you are deleting a factory-configured logical unit, FIRST 
     determine the size of one drive in your array subsystem (or 
     integrated array).  You will need this information when you 
     create a logical unit or modify logical unit parameters. See 
     the User Guide for more information.
 
     CAUTION: Copy the data on a logical unit to back-up media 
              file-by-file BEFORE deleting the logical unit.  
              Once the logical unit is deleted, you can NOT access 
              the logical unit - the data is lost.
 
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     IMPORTANT NOTE:  If you are using UNIX 2.xx.xx, you must always
                      have a LUN 0 to access ANY logical unit.  This
                      means if you delete LUN 0, you must recreate a
                      LUN 0 before quitting ace.
 
     NOTE:  The procedures for changing the RAID level or drive 
            grouping include a number of UNIX V.4 tasks, which vary 
            depending on the version of UNIX V.4 you are using.  
            See the User Guide for the exact steps to follow.
 
 
     PROCEDURE -- DELETING A LOGICAL UNIT 
 
     1.   Back-up the data on the logical unit you want to delete 
          by copying it file-by-file to the back-up media (if 
          necessary).
 
     2.   Select the desired logical unit with the arrow keys.  If you 
          are using UNIX V.4 2.xx.xx AND plan to delete LUN 0, select
          LUN 0 NOW.
 
     3.   Press "d" to delete the logical unit.
                                (CONTINUED)
     4.   Press "c" to confirm that you want to continue with the 
          logical unit deletion, or press "q" to quit the deletion.
 
      Are you changing the RAID level or drive grouping for the
      logical unit, using UNIX V.4 2.xx.xx, AND deleted LUN 0?
 
       YES
       Create LUN 0 NOW, then go to step 6. -->
 
       NO 
       Go to step 5. -->
 
     5.   Repeat steps 3 - 4 until you have deleted all the logical 
          units you want to delete.
 
     6.   Press "q" to quit ace.
 
     7.   Press <F3> (CONT) to run mkdtab or mktable.
 
          This will take some time.
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
      Are you changing the RAID level or drive grouping for the
      logical unit, using UNIX V.4 2.xx.xx, AND deleted LUN 0?
 
       YES
       Go to step 5, and complete the procedure. -->
 
       NO 
       Go to step 8. -->
 
     8.   Edit the following file by removing the logical unit(s)
          you deleted:
 
               /etc/vfstable
 
     9.   Delete the drive you want to delete, or recreate the logical
          unit with the new RAID level or drive grouping.
 
